Build firstCopies

Keep the first copy of each number in `readings`. Skip any later copies of the same number. Keep the order in which the numbers first appear. For [4, 2, 4, 3, 2], `firstCopies` is [4, 2, 3].

Return the answer

Find the smallest number in `firstCopies`. Store it in `smallest`. Complete `firstArrivalsFloorTicket` so it returns `smallest`. Return it, do not print it.

Why this works

Each value in `firstCopies` follows task 1. The later tasks use only those values. Keeping these steps separate helps avoid returning the list when the question asks for one number. Check empty input, one item, repeated values, and the largest allowed input.

Efficiency goal: time O(n); extra memory, including the helper list, O(n). n is the input length. These labels describe how work and memory grow with the input.

javascript
function firstArrivalsFloorTicket(readings) {
  const a=readings; let out=[];
  const seen=new Set();for(const x of a)if(!seen.has(x)){seen.add(x);out.push(x);}
  return out.length?Math.min(...out):0;
}


